# Target C — Section 124: Type-II near-disk geometry localizes loaded unit contacts

**Date:** 2026-09-12
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**
**Status:** proof-only research candidate on theorem-state `2026-09-12.audit.46`.
**Dependencies:** canonical Sections 63 and 80.

The canonical Type-II branch gives a loaded force hull `F_D` which, after translation
and scaling by `D`, converges to the diameter-one disk. This section extracts an exact
combinatorial consequence of that convergence:

\[
\boxed{
 b,b'\in B,\ |b-b'|=1
 \Longrightarrow
 b,b'\text{ are consecutive vertices of }F_D
}
\tag{124.1}
\]

for all sufficiently large Type-II survivors. In the exact Section-63 notation,

\[
\boxed{c_B=0.}
\tag{124.2}
\]

Thus the loaded-loaded unit-contact graph is eventually a subgraph of the cyclic loaded
hull graph. The theorem does not use any post-123 carrier coefficient.

## 124.1 Finite near-disk chord lemma

Let `K` be a convex polygon and suppose

\[
d_H(K,B(o,R))\le\delta<R.
\tag{124.3}
\]

Translate `o` to the origin and put

\[
r=R-\delta,
\qquad R_+=R+\delta.
\]

For convex compact sets Hausdorff distance equals uniform support-function distance, so

\[
B(0,r)\subset K\subset B(0,R_+).
\tag{124.4}
\]

Let

\[
\beta=\arccos(r/R_+).
\tag{124.5}
\]

If `x in partial K` and `u` is any outward unit supporting normal at `x`, then

\[
u\cdot x=h_K(u)\ge r,
\qquad |x|\le R_+,
\]

hence

\[
\angle(u,x)\le\beta.
\tag{124.6}
\]

Now let `p,q` be vertices with `|p-q|=1`. Since `|p|,|q|>=r`, their smaller radial
angle `gamma` satisfies

\[
1=|p-q|\ge2r\sin(\gamma/2),
\]

so

\[
\gamma\le2\arcsin(1/(2r)).
\tag{124.7}
\]

Because `0` lies in the interior of `K`, radial projection preserves cyclic order on
`partial K`. Choose the boundary arc from `p` to `q` whose polar angle runs through the
shorter radial interval. Every point of every side on this arc has radial angle in that
interval. By (124.6), all outward side normals, and hence all oriented side directions,
lie in one angular interval of width at most

\[
\tau
:=2\arccos(r/R_+)+2\arcsin(1/(2r)).
\tag{124.8}
\]

### Lemma 124.1

Assume every side of `K` has length at least one and `tau<2pi/3`. Then every unit
chord between vertices of `K` is a polygon side.

### Proof

If unit-separated vertices `p,q` were nonconsecutive, the selected short-polar arc
would have at least two side vectors `v_1,...,v_j`, `j>=2`. Let `w` be the midpoint
unit direction of their common direction interval. Then

\[
\begin{aligned}
1=|q-p|
&\ge w\cdot(q-p)\\
&=\sum_iw\cdot v_i\\
&\ge\sum_i|v_i|\cos(\tau/2)\\
&\ge2\cos(\tau/2)>1,
\end{aligned}
\]

contradiction. `square`

## 124.2 Type-II application

Canonical Section 80 gives centers `o_D` such that

\[
\delta_D:=d_H(F_D,B(o_D,D/2))=o(D).
\tag{124.9}
\]

Set

\[
r_D=D/2-\delta_D,
\qquad R_{+,D}=D/2+\delta_D.
\]

Then

\[
r_D/R_{+,D}\to1,
\qquad r_D\to\infty,
\]

and therefore the quantity in (124.8) tends to zero. Every side of `F_D` joins two
source points and has length at least one. Lemma 124.1 applies for all sufficiently
large `D`, proving (124.1).

Canonical Section 63 defines

\[
c_B=e_{BB}-(q-m),
\]

where `m` is the number of nonunit loaded-hull sides. Since every loaded-loaded unit
contact is now a unit hull side,

\[
\boxed{c_B=0,
\qquad e_{BB}=q-m.}
\tag{124.10}
\]

The exact Section-63 identities specialize to

\[
\boxed{e_{BB}=Q+2k+u_B,}
\tag{124.11}
\]

\[
\boxed{c_I+s_C=m+k-2,}
\tag{124.12}
\]

where `k=d_H+s_Z` and `u_B=x_B-m`.

A local corollary will be used next: every loaded-loaded unit contact with both
endpoints in one attachment set `A_r` is a side of the global force hull, never an
internal loaded chord.

## 124.3 Hostile checks

1. The proof needs only `delta_D/D->0`; `delta_D` itself may diverge.
2. It uses the short **polar** arc, so there is no ambiguity from a long complementary
   Gauss-map arc.
3. The normal estimate (124.6) holds at every point of each side, not only at endpoint
   normal cones.
4. The strict threshold `2pi/3` is safe because the Type-II angular width tends to
   zero.
5. One unit hull side is allowed; the theorem forbids only a nonconsecutive unit chord.
6. Nonunit hull sides and shallow two-edge outer detours remain possible.
7. Contact-isolated source points are untouched. No full-source ZR/ZF statement is
   used.

**Conclusion:** Section 124 is a proof-only Type-II structural theorem. C-II remains
open.
